\section*{Kobayashi Pseudometric Vanishing on Calabi–Yau Manifolds}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Every complex K3 surface is claimed to satisfy the Oka property, including convex approximation in arbitrary source dimension. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 042, 2026-09-23): Local sprays and quantitative sewing propagate complete directions across long annular chains without losing transverse width. Polynomial changes of source coordinates convert parameter-dependent strip approximation into convex approximation, and the corrected period-orbit alternatives transfer the construction to every K3 period.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 042, 2026-09-23): Convex approximation makes every complex K3 surface Oka; the dense-entire-curve corollary in particular supplies entire curves through any point and tangent, giving zero Kobayashi pseudometric in that Calabi-Yau surface class. Higher-dimensional compact Calabi-Yau manifolds are not covered.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 042 has no Lean scope page in this snapshot; no formalization of these manuscript results is listed.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

